I'm proposing some voluntary conducts.  I'm particularly pleased with
the level of challenge presented by ascetic conduct and the simplicity
of nudist.  The last one, magic-free, is clearly way too hard to be
practicable, but hey, maybe somebody's much better at the game than I
am and looking for an extreme challenge.

Ascetic:
 * You drink nothing but water.  (Whether it's blessed/holy doesn't
matter.)  No quaffing potions, except water.
 * You don't drink from fountains directly.  (You CAN dip potions you
don't need, which will probably
   be most of them, to convert them to water.)  Drinking from sinks
_risks_ violating the conduct, as there's
   a small chance that what comes out will be a random potion other
than water.  (If what comes out is water,
   even if it's hard water or sewer water or even toxic waste, you're
still ascetic; but if what comes out is a
   potion of booze or speed or what-have-you, you've violated the
conduct.)
 * You eat only when weak with hunger, and never so much that you
aren't hungry any more.  (If you become
   not hungry any more by a means other than eating, e.g., via prayer,
that's fine.  Prayer and fasting is
   fully compatible with an ascetic lifestyle.)
 * You never eat anything that tastes good.  (There's some room for
interpretation here, but if the game
   consistently tells you that a particular thing "tastes terrible",
you're definitely allowed to eat it, subject
   to the aforementioned hunger restriction.  Bland food, such as
rations, is probably acceptable to all
   but the strictest ascetics.)
 * You have no direct dealings (melee contact) with any incubus,
succubus, or nymph.  You CAN
   hit them with ranged attacks (if you aren't also going for
pacifist), let your pet handle them, or
   just avoid them.
 * You are never healed or otherwise aided by a nurse.  (Fighting a
nurse while fully clothed is okay.)
 * Although nethack never holds you responsible for your pet's
actions, you must not actively
   contribute to any hedonistic behavior on your pet's part; e.g., you
don't throw tasty treats
   to your pet or drop them right next to it, because a proper ascetic
wouldn't eat such fare.
   (Your pet will invariably find things laying around that you don't
approve of and eat them before
   you can do anything about it, but that's not your fault.)

Nazarite:
 * You totally avoid dead bodies of all kinds (except plants, fungi,
and such).  That means you can't
   pick up a (non-vegetarian) corpse, even with gloves.  A fully
strict Nazarite wouldn't
   even voluntarily be in the same building with a corpse, but for
Nethack purposes I think it's
   good enough not to voluntarily step onto the same tile with one or
pick one up.
 * You drink no alcohol (so, no potions of booze) and do not eat or
drink anything derived from grapes.
   Since Nethack does not differentiate various types of fruit juice,
you just don't drink the fruit juice,
   in case it might contain some traces of grape.  If the game had
grapes or raisins or any other
   grape-related thing in it, you wouldn't eat that either.  You CAN
eat other fruit (like apples), though --
   unless you're also going for some other conduct that forbids it.
 * You never touch a razor and never cut your hair.  (In game terms, I
think this is a freebie,
   since AFAIK such things aren't implemented anyway.)
 * You don't wear comfortable clothing.  (I doubt if priests' or
monks' robes would be a problem here.
   Probably best not to wear the Hawaiian shirt, though.  If the game
had silk, it would be verboten.)

Nudist:
 * You remove your starting clothing immediately (before moving
around).
 * You never use the Wear or Puton actions, obviously.
 * If at any time you find yourself wearing clothing for any reason,
you take it off immediately,
   definitely before you start walking around again.  Anything you
could don with the Wear or Puton
   actions counts as clothing.

Magic-Free:
 * This means you don't use magic voluntarily; magic will of course
happen TO you all the
    time in this game; that's not your fault; you are responsible only
for your own conduct.
 * You don't quaff, throw, or otherwise use, as they stand, any
magical potions.  Fruit juice, booze,
   water, sickness (which according to the wiki is just spoiled
juice), acid and oil are all okay to
   use, as they are non-magical (UNLESS they are milky or smoky).  You
MAY dip the potions
   in water (fountain or sink) until they're just water, and then you
can use the water.
   Similarly, if there's an alchemical recipe that will allow you to
convert magic potions into
   non-magical ones, you're allowed to do that and use the non-magical
results.
 * You don't zap any magic wands.  (Being hit with them by enemies
doesn't count against you.)
 * You don't engrave anything with wands that have engraving-related
magic (e.g., fire, lightning).
   If you have one that's basically just a stylus for engraving
purposes, that's okay.
 * You don't break any magic wands for their magical effect.
(Breaking a wand with no magical
   effect when broken, just to get rid of it so enemies don't use it,
is fine.)
 * You don't use magical bags (holding, tricks).  Oilskin and regular
sacks are okay.
 * You don't cast any spells or use any magical spellbooks or scrolls
for their magical effects.
   You MAY carry them to shops and sell them, and you MAY write your
name on blank ones,
   though I'm not sure what the purpose would be.  (A "magic marker"
isn't magical to any
   significant extent, so you're allowed to use it.)
 * You don't use magic rings.
 * You don't walk into a known polymorph trap.  If you inadvertently
step into an unknown one and
    become something with obviously magical abilities, you don't
voluntarily use the magical abilities
    (e.g., a dragon's breath attacks).  You MAY use the ordinary
garden-variety non-magical abilities of
    whatever you have been turned into (e.g., a giant's strength), and
you aren't responsible for things
    that happen involuntarily (e.g., passive attacks).
 * Just in general, you don't use magic.  You do everything by plain
old ordinary non-magical means.
 * I suspect fully strict adherence to magic-free would rule out any
ending to the game other than your
   death, so some special accommodations may need to be made to allow
certain mandatory game tasks
   to be completed (as is also the case with some other conducts,
e.g., atheist and illiterate).

And yes, I'm completely aware that combining these with eachother or
with other existing conducts could well make the game completely
unplayable.  Ascetic with Nazarite and Atheist would be a particularly
impossible combination, and Magic-Free is way too hard to combine with
anything.  Nudist could be interesting to combine with some existing
conducts, though.


